## Best Practices

1. **Always set supplier_id** when creating stock items from suppliers
2. **Use create_supplier_transaction** when adding initial stock or restocking from suppliers
3. **Provide purchase_price** when adding quantity to update price tracking
4. **Use repeatBySerial** for quick restocking of common orders
5. **Monitor price tracking** to identify price trends and negotiate better deals
6. **Link stock items to suppliers** for better inventory management

## Summary

- ✅ Stock items can be linked to suppliers
- ✅ Automatic supplier transaction creation when adding stock
- ✅ Price tracking (last, highest, lowest purchase prices)
- ✅ **Payment account selection** - Choose which account to pay from (any account type)
- ✅ **Direct money deduction** - Selected account is debited (money deducted directly)
- ✅ Quick restock via serial number
- ✅ Supplier history automatically maintained

## Important Notes

⚠️ **Money Deduction:** When you create a stock purchase with `deposit_account_id`, the money is **deducted** from that account. Make sure the account has sufficient balance.

⚠️ **Account Selection:**
- **You can select ANY account** in the system (cash, bank, revenue, expense, etc.)
- Always provide `deposit_account_id` to explicitly choose payment account
- Use `GET /api/accounts?is_active=true` to get all available accounts
- If not provided, system uses `payment_method` to determine (cash/bank)
- Defaults to cash account if neither is provided

⚠️ **Direct Deduction:**
- **Selected deposit account** (any account type) is **debited** (balance decreases)
- Money is **directly deducted** - no expense account is credited
- Transaction is linked to the `SupplierTransaction` record

⚠️ **Account Types:**
- You can pay from **any account type**: cash, bank, revenue, expense, receivable, advance, etc.
- The system will **deduct money directly** from whichever account you specify
- Use `GET /api/accounts` to see all available accounts with their balances

